1
00:00:02,276 --> 00:00:04,199
So what can we say about comments?

2
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:06,475
Well, just one main thing.

3
00:00:07,069 --> 00:00:08,960
Typically, you wanna avoid them.

4
00:00:09,553 --> 00:00:11,509
Of course, there are a couple of exceptions

5
00:00:11,509 --> 00:00:11,717
though.

6
00:00:12,535 --> 00:00:15,628
But indeed, a lot of comments are just bad.

7
00:00:16,205 --> 00:00:18,337
For example, because they simply convey

8
00:00:18,626 --> 00:00:19,956
redundant information.

9
00:00:20,549 --> 00:00:22,329
Have a look at this code snippet.

10
00:00:22,874 --> 00:00:24,701
The comments you see here are pretty much

11
00:00:24,701 --> 00:00:27,266
redundant. For example, this comment. The

12
00:00:27,282 --> 00:00:29,092
database engine to which we wanna connect.

13
00:00:29,669 --> 00:00:32,152
Well, if that's what we have here, why don't

14
00:00:32,152 --> 00:00:34,299
we just name it dbEngine

15
00:00:35,676 --> 00:00:37,663
and use that name in our code

16
00:00:38,897 --> 00:00:40,162
instead of dbDriver?

17
00:00:41,123 --> 00:00:42,469
Also, these comments here,

18
00:00:43,062 --> 00:00:46,250
connect to the SQL driver if driver is set

19
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:49,262
to SQL. Well, this should be obvious. If you

20
00:00:49,294 --> 00:00:51,969
use proper names as we now do in this

21
00:00:51,969 --> 00:00:54,645
example, you shouldn't need any explanatory

22
00:00:54,741 --> 00:00:56,615
comments because the names should be

23
00:00:56,615 --> 00:00:58,121
self-explanatory already.

24
00:00:58,922 --> 00:01:01,294
And this code reads just fine without

25
00:01:01,326 --> 00:01:03,825
comments. Actually, the comments even make

26
00:01:03,825 --> 00:01:05,860
it a bit harder to understand it because

27
00:01:05,908 --> 00:01:08,584
there is extra text to read. And you will

28
00:01:08,696 --> 00:01:11,003
probably not skip this text if you go

29
00:01:11,019 --> 00:01:13,166
through the code because it might contain

30
00:01:13,166 --> 00:01:15,505
important information. So here you have a

31
00:01:15,505 --> 00:01:18,005
great example where comments actually make

32
00:01:18,005 --> 00:01:19,799
it harder to understand the code.

33
00:01:20,632 --> 00:01:22,731
Another kind of bad comments would be

34
00:01:22,956 --> 00:01:26,000
dividers or comments that simply mark a

35
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:26,849
block of code.

36
00:01:27,684 --> 00:01:30,045
Again, here's an example from the same file.

37
00:01:30,656 --> 00:01:33,515
I got these big markers here which basically

38
00:01:33,531 --> 00:01:36,246
just inform the developer going through this

39
00:01:36,342 --> 00:01:38,415
code file that we're defining a couple of

40
00:01:38,431 --> 00:01:40,390
globals and then a couple of classes.

41
00:01:41,162 --> 00:01:43,523
Well, again, this should be obvious. I mean,

42
00:01:43,668 --> 00:01:45,499
we don't have to tell anyone that we're

43
00:01:45,515 --> 00:01:47,683
defining a couple of classes. We can tell by

44
00:01:47,683 --> 00:01:50,238
the class keyword. So comments like this

45
00:01:50,318 --> 00:01:52,712
also should be redundant. And if you feel

46
00:01:52,744 --> 00:01:55,088
the need for introducing them, then you

47
00:01:55,088 --> 00:01:57,591
might simply have too much code in one file

48
00:01:58,024 --> 00:01:59,757
and you might wanna consider splitting your

49
00:01:59,869 --> 00:02:02,067
code across multiple files instead.

50
00:02:02,837 --> 00:02:05,355
Amongst the worst kinds of comments are

51
00:02:05,419 --> 00:02:06,591
misleading comments.

52
00:02:07,328 --> 00:02:09,671
For example, consider this comment here.

53
00:02:10,296 --> 00:02:12,301
We say that we're updating a user, but

54
00:02:12,301 --> 00:02:14,291
actually the name implies that we're

55
00:02:14,387 --> 00:02:16,617
inserting a user, which is not the same as

56
00:02:16,713 --> 00:02:19,135
updating. So what are we doing? What the

57
00:02:19,135 --> 00:02:22,135
comment says or what the name says? So all

58
00:02:22,264 --> 00:02:24,767
of a sudden, a name which was good now is a

59
00:02:24,767 --> 00:02:27,094
name we have to question, and we have to

60
00:02:27,158 --> 00:02:29,373
dive into the insert method to see what it

61
00:02:29,405 --> 00:02:29,935
really does.

62
00:02:30,833 --> 00:02:32,502
So that's a great example where a comment

63
00:02:32,534 --> 00:02:33,610
does not help us at all.

64
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:36,289
If the insert method would really be

65
00:02:36,306 --> 00:02:38,584
updating a user, we of course should rename

66
00:02:38,632 --> 00:02:41,425
it. And if we can't rename it because it's

67
00:02:41,425 --> 00:02:44,699
from some badly written third-party library

68
00:02:45,036 --> 00:02:47,138
or for whatever reason, then we should at

69
00:02:47,138 --> 00:02:49,417
least update the comment to be something

70
00:02:49,465 --> 00:02:51,615
like this, where it now really makes it

71
00:02:51,615 --> 00:02:53,541
clear that the comment is right and not the

72
00:02:53,541 --> 00:02:53,894
method.

73
00:02:54,424 --> 00:02:56,671
Of course, we should still rename the method

74
00:02:56,687 --> 00:02:57,810
as soon as possible though.

75
00:02:58,789 --> 00:03:00,923
Now another practice which you see quite

76
00:03:00,923 --> 00:03:04,069
often is commented out code. And I will say,

77
00:03:04,294 --> 00:03:06,717
I myself do comment out code, for example in

78
00:03:06,717 --> 00:03:08,964
some of my other courses, to keep it around

79
00:03:08,964 --> 00:03:12,382
for reference or to, well, keep it in sight

80
00:03:12,414 --> 00:03:14,629
whilst we maybe replace it with new code.

81
00:03:14,982 --> 00:03:17,373
I'm fine with doing that, but you shouldn't

82
00:03:17,470 --> 00:03:20,005
permanently comment out code. If you really

83
00:03:20,102 --> 00:03:22,140
have code which you don't need anymore, you

84
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:24,692
should just remove it. Nowadays, we should

85
00:03:24,772 --> 00:03:27,228
be using version control systems like Git

86
00:03:27,404 --> 00:03:29,619
anyways, and therefore we can always bring

87
00:03:29,747 --> 00:03:32,604
back deleted code if we really need it. So

88
00:03:32,620 --> 00:03:35,220
don't be afraid of deleting code, just do it

89
00:03:35,220 --> 00:03:36,327
if you don't need it anymore.

90
00:03:37,194 --> 00:03:39,072
So instead of keeping around code like this,

91
00:03:39,617 --> 00:03:40,645
we should just delete it.

92
00:03:41,367 --> 00:03:42,924
And then simply commit this change.

93
00:03:43,454 --> 00:03:45,606
And assuming that we also committed changes

94
00:03:45,638 --> 00:03:47,822
before we deleted it, we can always go back

95
00:03:47,822 --> 00:03:50,054
to these older states if we need that code

96
00:03:50,054 --> 00:03:50,343
again.
